---
title: "Server-side syntax highlighting"
---

Code is highlighted in Rust at render time (syntect → `tali-hl-` scope classes,
coloured by CSS), so a built page carries no highlighter and the light/dark toggle
restyles code without re-highlighting. This document pins the language coverage.

## Languages syntect bundles

```rust
pub fn highlight(code: &str, lang: Option<&str>) -> String {
    let ss = syntaxes();
    match lang.map(alias).and_then(|t| ss.find_syntax_by_token(t)) {
        Some(syntax) => classed(code, syntax, ss),
        None => html_escape(code), // an unknown language degrades to plain text
    }
}
```

```python
def entropy(p):
    """Shannon entropy in bits."""
    return -sum(x * log2(x) for x in p if x > 0)
```

## Languages the bundled set omits

Neither of these resolves in syntect's default syntax set, so both rendered as
unstyled plain text until the `bat`-curated extras were loaded. The docs use them,
so they are pinned here.

```ts
// A TypeScript fence: `ts` aliases to `typescript`.
export function mount(root: HTMLElement, opts: MountOptions = {}): void {
    const panels = root.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>(".tali-panel");
    panels.forEach((p, i) => p.setAttribute("data-index", String(i)));
}
```

```toml
[workspace.dependencies]
syntect = { version = "5", default-features = false, features = ["default-fancy"] }
two-face = { version = "0.4", default-features = false }
```

## Fences that stay plain on purpose

`text` and `console` resolve to no syntax deliberately: a transcript or a chunk of
sample output is not code, so it must render unhighlighted *without* `taliesin check`
reporting it as an unknown language.

```text
the quick brown fox
jumps over the lazy dog
```

```console
$ taliesin build docs/guide
  built  _site/index.html
```

An unlabelled fence is likewise plain, and likewise silent.

```
no language token at all
```

## A language plus an attribute, the ecosystem's spelling

mdBook, rustdoc, Pandoc, Docusaurus and GitHub all write ` ```rust,ignore ` — a language
followed by an attribute for a tool that consumes it. Taliesin consumes none of those
attributes, but the *language* is still the first token, so the block highlights as Rust
and `check` stays silent. Before this, the whole string became the class
(`language-rust,ignore`, which no CSS rule or highlighter can match), so real code from
migrated projects shipped as unstyled plain text.

```rust,ignore
fn main() {
    let unused: u32 = 1;
}
```

The same holds for a space-separated attribute and for more than one attribute.

```python should_panic
def f(x):
    return x + 1
```

```rust,no_run,edition2021
fn main() {}
```

## The registered languages that highlight but never execute

`vocab`'s cell-language registry carries four languages described as "highlighted only;
not executed" — `bash`, `sql`, `julia` and `rust`. They are a real part of the authoring
vocabulary (completion offers them, `check` accepts them), so a document that writes one
must come out coloured rather than plain, even though no kernel is ever consulted. `bash`
and `rust` are exercised above and in `tarn/`; these two are pinned here.

```julia
function loglikelihood(x::Vector{Float64}, mu::Float64, sigma::Float64)
    n = length(x)
    -n / 2 * log(2pi * sigma^2) - sum((x .- mu) .^ 2) / (2 * sigma^2)
end
```

```sql
SELECT
    region,
    count(*) AS n,
    round(avg(latency_ms), 1) AS mean_latency
FROM requests
WHERE created_at >= date('now', '-7 days')
GROUP BY region
HAVING count(*) > 100
ORDER BY mean_latency DESC;
```
